The user wants a program to find common elements in two arrays without extra data structures (no hash sets, etc.). So use nested loops O(n*m) approach, or sort in place then two pointers — sorting in place uses O(1) extra space (in-place sort). I'll provide both approaches, with code in Python and maybe Java/C. Keep it concise.|# Finding Common Elements in Two Arrays (Without Extra Data Structures)

## Approach 1: Brute Force (O(n×m) time, O(1) space)

Compare every element of the first array with every element of the second array. To avoid printing duplicates, we check if the element was already printed.

```python
def find_common(arr1, arr2):
    n = len(arr1)
    m = len(arr2)

    for i in range(n):
        # Check if arr1[i] was already printed (avoid duplicates)
        already_printed = False
        for k in range(i):
            if arr1[k] == arr1[i]:
                already_printed = True
                break

        if not already_printed:
            for j in range(m):
            if arr1[i] == arr2[j]:
                print(arr1[i])
                break
```

